E8 NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

This file is about the changes in each release of E8, newest first.
'C-h n' shows it; with a prefix argument, 'C-u C-h n' asks for a
release and shows only that one's changes.  TAB on a heading folds it.

A package that is a repository of its own keeps its news in a NEWS file
of the same form, which 'C-h P' shows under its description.


* Changes in E8 0.5.20

** 'C-m', 'C-i' and 'C-[' are 'RET', 'TAB' and 'ESC', as Emacs reads them.
A binding written either way runs from either key, in the terminal
and the GUI, so keys written as Emacs packages write them, such as
Org's 'C-c C-x C-i', can be typed.  'C-h c' then ctrl+m says RET, and
Meta-TAB is written 'C-M-i'.

** Org capture works as Org's does, to every target and of every type.
Entries, items, check items, table lines and plain text go to 'id',
'clock', 'file+regexp' and function targets too.  'C-u', 'C-0', 'C-1'
and 'C-c C-w' work, as do ':prepend', ':immediate-finish',
':clock-keep', ':time-prompt' and Org's escapes, '%^g' and '%^{PROP}p'
among them.

** Org archives subtrees.
'C-c C-x C-s' ('C-c $') moves a subtree to its archive location, under
a heading or in a date tree; 'C-c C-x A' files it under an Archive
sibling and 'C-c C-x a' toggles the ARCHIVE tag.  'C-u' and 'C-u C-u'
archive every finished or old tree, and an active region each heading.

** The agenda archives, shows archived trees and dims blocked tasks.
'$', 'a', 'C-c C-x A' and 'C-c C-x a' archive from the agenda.  'v a'
and 'v A' show archived trees and the archive files.  Tasks blocked by
their dependencies are dimmed, or hidden, and the week agenda leaves out
archived and COMMENT trees.

** Org has clock tables and dynamic blocks.
'C-c C-c' on a '#+BEGIN: clocktable' block, or 'M-x org-clock-report',
writes the table, with Org's scopes, time ranges, steps and options.
'S-<left>' and 'S-<right>' on its first line move its time range.
'C-c C-x x' inserts a dynamic block.

** Sparse trees, and matching by tags, properties and TODO states.
'C-c /' shows the matches of a regexp, a TODO state, a match, a
property or a date, and 'C-c \' those of a tags match, highlighted
until 'C-c C-c'; 'M-g n' and 'M-g p' move between them.  '/' in the
agenda's dispatcher searches the agenda files with 'multi-occur'.

** The agenda has its tags and search views.
'm' and 'M' in 'org-agenda' list the headings a tags match picks,
and 's' and 'S' search the agenda files for a phrase or for words and
regexps.  The TODO list reads the TODO ignore options, such as
'org-agenda-todo-ignore-scheduled'.

** Org's TODO dependencies.
With 'org-enforce-todo-dependencies', an entry cannot be marked done
while a child, or an earlier sibling under an ORDERED parent, is still
to do; checkboxes can block it too.  'C-c C-x o' toggles ORDERED, and
'BLOCKED' works in matches.

** 'C-c C-x C-d' shows each subtree's clocked time.
The file's total goes in the echo area.  'C-u' sums today's time and
'C-u C-u' asks for a range.

** Org's clock is kept from one session to the next.
With 'org-clock-persistence-insinuate', the running clock and the clock
history are saved when E8 exits, in the form Emacs writes.  Clocking in
on an entry with an open CLOCK line continues it.

** Clocking in offers recent tasks and shows the effort in the mode line.
'C-u C-c C-x C-i' picks from *Clock Task Select*.  The mode line shows
'[0:15/1:00]' and marks an overrun, and 'C-c C-x C-x' clocks in on the
last task.  The clock can start from the last clock-out, switch states,
and notify with a sound.

** The running clock follows its entry when it moves.
Moving a subtree with 'M-<up>' and 'M-<down>', cutting and pasting it,
refiling, archiving or sorting no longer loses the clock, which used to
fail with "Clock start time is gone".

** 'C-c ^' sorts entries, table lines and list items.
Entries by name, number, priority, property, TODO order or time;
tables by the column point is in; and lists by their items' first lines
or checkboxes.  A capital letter reverses the order.

** Radio buttons and ORDERED checkboxes in Org lists.
'C-c C-x C-r' ticks the item at point and unticks the rest of its list,
as do 'C-c C-c' and 'C-c C-x C-b' in a ':radio t' list.  Under an
ORDERED entry a box can be ticked only after the ones before it.

** 'C-c C-q' sets tags as Org does.
With keys in the tag table it shows Org's fast tag selection menu, with
groups; without them it completes each tag after a colon.  An active
region sets the tags of each heading in it.

** '#+SETUPFILE:' is read, and keyword lines are found as Org finds them.
Another file's TODO states, tags, '#+STARTUP:' and other keywords come
in.  An indented '#+TODO:' line counts, and one in an example block no
longer does.

** 'org-store-link' and 'C-c C-l' store and insert links as Org does.
Links are stored to headings, targets, other files, Dired and the
agenda; 'C-c C-l' offers the stored ones, and 'C-c M-l' inserts the
last.  ID links are stored as 'org-id-link-to-org-use-id' says.

** Org IDs are kept in 'org-id-locations-file', as Emacs keeps them.
Following an ID looks there first, in the form Emacs writes it.  New IDs
follow 'org-id-method' and 'org-id-prefix', and duplicate IDs are
warned of.

** Refiling works over a region and to new parent headings.
'org-reverse-note-order', 'org-refile-allow-creating-parent-nodes' and
'org-outline-path-complete-in-steps' are read.  'C-u C-u C-c C-w' goes
to the last refiled place, 'C-2' refiles to the clocked entry and 'C-3'
copies; 'org-agenda-refile' works from the agenda.

** Org's commands over a region reach every heading in it.
'C-c C-t', 'C-c C-q', 'C-c C-s', 'C-c C-d' and the archive commands
follow 'org-loop-over-headlines-in-active-region', 'start-level'
included.

** 'C-c C-j' ('org-goto') jumps to a heading, and isearch opens folds.
'org-goto' shows the outline to move about or search in.  'C-c %' and
'C-c &' use Org's mark ring.  Isearch opens folded text while a match
is in it, as 'search-invisible' 'open' does.

** Archived subtrees stay closed, and 'org-cycle-hook' runs.
TAB and S-TAB run Org's cycle hooks.  'C-c C-<tab>' opens an archived
subtree anyway, and '#+STARTUP: showall' now shows everything.

** Org properties from 'org-global-properties', and 'NAME+' values.
A 'NAME+' line adds to a property's value, and inheritance follows
Org's, through '#+PROPERTY:' lines and 'org-global-properties'.

** Tangling expands noweb references and writes ':comments'.
A reference is replaced by the body of the heading or block it names,
or of every block with that ':noweb-ref'.  'C-c C-c' expands them too.

** Org's block motion and effort values.
'C-c M-f' and 'C-c M-b' move between blocks.  'C-c C-x e' offers an
entry's allowed efforts, and 'C-c C-x E' steps to the next.

** 'C-c ,' sets a priority as Org does, with one key.
'SPC' removes the cookie, and a key outside 'org-priority-highest' to
'org-priority-lowest' is refused; with a lowest below 65 priorities
are numbers.  'C-u C-c ,' shows the priority.  In the agenda '+', '-',
'S-<up>' and 'S-<down>' raise and lower it.  Called from code,
'org-priority' now takes a character's code, as Org's does, not a
letter as a string.

** Agenda lines leave out the timestamp whose time they show.
',' sets a priority from the agenda again, and point keeps its place
when a line is written again.

** 'completing-read-multiple' and warnings as Emacs has them.
'completing-read-multiple' completes each answer between separators,
and 'display-warning', 'warn' and 'lwarn' write to *Warnings*.

** Transient menus take more of transient's keys.
'?' shows help and 'q' comes back from it, 'C-z' and 'M-x
transient-resume' suspend and resume, and 'C-M-p' and 'C-M-n' go
through earlier values.  '<up>' and '<down>' move between suffixes,
which 'M-RET' or a click runs.

** The markdown-mode port is listed among the ports.
'e8-markdown-mode' gives '.md' files a mode, in a repository of its
own.


* Changes in E8 0.5.19

** Org can ask for a note when a state changes, in *Org Note*.
A keyword with '@', as 'WAIT(w@)', opens *Org Note* to write why; 'C-c
C-c' stores the note and 'C-c C-k' leaves it out.  'C-c C-z' adds a
note to the entry at any time.  Notes go into the LOGBOOK drawer when
'org-log-into-drawer' or a LOG_INTO_DRAWER property asks for one.

** Org's logging is set as Org sets it.
The LOGGING property, inherited from a parent entry, the '#+STARTUP:'
words 'logdone', 'lognotedone', 'logrepeat', 'logreschedule',
'logredeadline', 'logrefile', 'lognoteclock-out', 'logdrawer',
'logstatesreversed' and the rest, and '#+PROPERTY:' lines all decide
what is logged.  With 'org-log-states-order-reversed' nil, notes are
kept oldest first.

** Org logs rescheduling, deadline changes, refiling and clocking out.
As 'org-log-reschedule', 'org-log-redeadline', 'org-log-refile' and
'org-log-note-clock-out' ask, with a time or a note.  'C-u C-u C-c C-s'
and 'C-u C-u C-c C-d' set a delay or a warning period, as in Org.

** 'org-adapt-indentation' works, both t and 'headline-data'.
Text under a heading is indented to its level, and moves with the
heading when it is promoted or demoted; with 'headline-data' only the
planning line and drawers are.

** 'C-c C-,' offers block templates in *Org Select*, as Org does.
Templates whose keys start alike are grouped under their first key.
A menu taller than its window scrolls with 'C-n', 'C-p', 'C-v' and
'M-v'.

** 'M-q' in Org fills as 'org-fill-paragraph' does.
Paragraphs, list items, comments and source blocks each fill in their
own way.

** Shell and comint buffers show ANSI colours as Emacs does.
'ansi-color.el' is ported: the eight colours, 256 colours and RGB,
and faint, blink and inverse text.  A coloured prompt keeps its
colours.  'comint-use-prompt-regexp' works, and TAB completes file
names at the prompt, and in shell-mode environment variables and
commands too.

** Commands disabled for new users ask first, as in Emacs.
'C-x n n', 'C-x C-u', 'C-x C-l', 'C-x <' and the rest say why they are
disabled and offer to run them, or to enable them for good in init.ts.

** Isearch searches for words and symbols, and edits its string.
'M-s w', 'M-s _' and 'M-s .' start or toggle word and symbol search.
A space matches any run of whitespace, toggled by 'M-s SPC'.  'M-e'
edits the search string, 'M-p' and 'M-n' bring back earlier searches,
and 'isearch-lazy-count' shows which match is current.

** 'zap-to-char' reads its character in the minibuffer, as Emacs 30's.
'M-z M-p' zaps to the same character again.

** Abbrevs are saved and read in Emacs's 'abbrev_defs' format.
An abbrev file written by Emacs reads in E8.  E8 offers to save
changed abbrevs, and a mode's abbrevs work in modes built on it.

** 'multi-occur' searches several buffers.
'multi-occur-in-matching-buffers' picks them by name, and 'M-s a C-o'
in the buffer list searches the marked ones.

** Programs run with 'process-environment' and are found along 'exec-path'.
'setenv' and 'getenv' are commands.  'call-process' takes Emacs's
arguments.

** Auto-revert as Emacs has it.
'auto-revert-mode', 'auto-revert-tail-mode' and
'global-auto-revert-mode' are told of changes as files change, and
can revert Dired and the buffer list too.

** Imenu keeps its index until *Rescan*, and can be on the menu bar.

** Dired guesses commands for '!' and '&', and 'Q' replaces in the marked files.
'* c' and 'M-DEL' read their marks as a key.  Failures are logged in
*Dired log*, which '?' shows.  wdired can change permissions.

** Saving text the file's coding cannot write asks which coding to use.
As Emacs does, listing the characters in *Warning*.  'C-x RET l' sets
a language environment, and a file's coding is found from XML and
HTML declarations, PO headers and LaTeX's inputenc.

** Characters by their Unicode names.
'C-x 8 RET' inserts a character by name or code, and 'C-u C-x ='
describes the character at point, as Emacs does.

** TAB again scrolls a long *Completions* list.
'scroll-up' and 'scroll-down' failed when called from code.


* Changes in E8 0.5.18

** 'C-M-i' ('completion-at-point') completes in the buffer, as Emacs does.
As far as the completion styles go, orderless's too; when they go no
further, the completions are listed in *Completions* below the window.
'M-<down>' and 'M-<up>' move among them, 'M-RET' puts one in, and 'TAB'
completes again; RET or a click in the list chooses too.  Leaving the
text, or 'C-g', takes the list away.  It used to ask in the minibuffer.

** *Completions* shows what a completion function says about each.
Its annotation goes after each completion, the function's order and
groups are kept, and with 'completion-cycle-threshold' set each try
puts in the next completion, as Emacs's 'minibuffer-force-complete'.

** eglot's completion shows the server's detail for each completion.
They come in the server's order, and once one is chosen, the edits it
brings are made: completing a Go package not yet imported adds its
import.

** Org reads the whole buffer under a narrowing, as Org does.
Narrowed to part of an entry with 'C-x n e' or 'C-x n n', RET and TAB
in a list item or a property drawer indent as in Org, and 'C-RET',
'C-c C-x M-w' and dragging an element with 'M-<up>' and 'M-<down>' act
as Org's do.

** Commands that act on tables leave a bar line in a block alone.
'M-<up>', 'C-c -', 'S-TAB' and the other commands that treat a line
starting with '|' as a table row ask the parser, as Org does, so such a
line in a source or example block is no table.

** 'M-h' in Org marks the element at point ('org-mark-element').

** 'C-M-\' ('indent-region') indents a narrowed buffer as Emacs does.
It widens around the mode's indenting, so a block whose first line is
outside the narrowing is indented whole.


* Changes in E8 0.5.17

** TypeScript files have a mode: 'typescript-ts-mode', and 'tsx-ts-mode'.
'.ts' and '.tsx' files, init.ts among them, are highlighted and
indented as Emacs's typescript-ts-mode does it, and the sexp and defun
commands follow their syntax tree.  TypeScript's grammar ships with E8.
Org's 'typescript' blocks get its faces, and 'C-c '' edits them in it.

** 'C-c C-c' in an Org source block runs it.
A TypeScript block runs in the editor, as an Emacs Lisp block runs in
Emacs, and its value goes under it after '#+RESULTS:', as Org writes
it.  'org-confirm-babel-evaluate' says whether to ask first, and
':results silent', 'none', 'append' and 'prepend' work as in Org.
'C-c C-v e' runs a block too.

** A configuration can be a literate Org file.
'org-babel-load-file' tangles the TypeScript blocks of an Org file into
a .ts file of the same name, when the Org file is newer, and loads it.
An init.ts of one line loads a 'config.org':

    e8.call("org-babel-load-file", e8.expandFileName("config.org", e8.variable("user-emacs-directory") as string));

** init.ts and 'load-file' can import packages.
A file that imports or exports runs as a module, a new one each time it
is loaded, so 'import { truthy } from "subr"' works in a configuration.

** The agent's conversation in 'agent-shell' looks like a chat.
Who speaks shows as a coloured name, each tool call as a mark for its
status and what it does, the agent's thinking and tool calls between
replies as one folded line ("Thought, ran 2 commands, edited a file")
that TAB opens, code as a captioned panel, and a spinner turns while
the agent works.  The buffer's text is still Org.


* Changes in E8 0.5.16

** 'C-h n' ('view-e8-news') shows E8's news, what changed in each release.
'view-emacs-news' is another name for it, as configurations written for
Emacs call it.

** 'C-h P' ('describe-package') describes a package.
For a package installed from a repository: its version and commit, what
it requires and what requires it, its README, and its news, from the
NEWS file in its repository.  For a package that ships with E8: what its
file says it is.

** Outlines no longer take a line starting with "f" for a heading.
'outline-regexp' matches a star or a form feed, as Emacs's does, so
folding in 'outline-mode' and 'outline-minor-mode' hides such lines.


* Changes in E8 0.5.15

** 'yank-from-kill-ring' offers what another program copied.
Text on the clipboard joins the kill ring before the choices are shown,
as Emacs's 'read-from-kill-ring' takes it in.


* Changes in E8 0.5.14

** 'C-x ESC ESC' and 'C-x M-:' edit and run an earlier command again.
'repeat-complex-command' takes it from 'command-history', which now
keeps every command run with 'M-x'.

** 'C-x C-n' sets a goal column for 'C-n' and 'C-p'.
'C-u C-x C-n' unsets it.

** More of Emacs's commands and keys.
'C-x C-r', 'C-x 4 r' and 'C-x 5 r' visit a file read-only.  'M-<next>',
'M-<prior>', 'M-<home>' and 'M-<end>' act in the next window.  New
commands 'rename-uniquely' and 'binary-overwrite-mode'.  'M-c' in
isearch toggles case folding, and 'C-u M-g g' goes to a line in the
other buffer.

** A negative argument makes the replace commands replace backward.
'C--' before 'M-%', 'C-M-%', 'replace-string' or 'replace-regexp'
replaces from point back to the start, and the 'y', 'n', '!', '.', ','
and '^' answers work backward too.  Prompts and messages name the
replacement as Emacs's do, such as "Query replace backward: ", and
'C-M-%' now says "regexp".

** Editing keeps within the narrowing, as in Emacs.
'delete-region', replacing and undo refuse positions outside it with
'args-out-of-range', and 'erase-buffer' widens.  'M-j', filling, 'C-c -',
dragging Org elements and 'M-<up>' keep to it, and Org, the agenda,
capture, refile and the clock widen where Emacs widens.  Saving a
narrowed buffer adds the final newline at the end of the file.

** Columns count control characters as Emacs does.
They take two columns, or four for C1 characters, and the mode line's
'%c' and scrolling's goal column count screen columns.

** Fixes in editing and movement.
'replace-match' converts case as Emacs's does, finding words by the
syntax table.  Overwrite mode follows Emacs, and 'C-q' reads digits
there.  Word motion stops where the script changes, 'count-words'
counts words in every script, and transposing next to emoji no longer
garbles text.  'M-<' and 'M->' with a count use tenths of the
narrowing; 'delete-indentation' works on the region; 'M-0 M-z' kills
nothing; DEL on the region is an undo step of its own; a third
'C-SPC' activates the mark; 'kill-ring-max' is obeyed.  'M-v' at the
top keeps point, scrolling by a count past the end fails without
scrolling, and 'C-l' and 'M-r' cap 'scroll-margin' and take fractions.

** Fixes in the minibuffer and keys.
An undefined key uses up the prefix argument.  'M-n' past the input
brings the default, and RET on empty input takes a default that is not
one of the completions.  'read-buffer' shows its default and hides
internal buffers.  'query-replace' offers the last pair and takes 'C-u'
for whole words.

** Fixes in Org.
Rescheduling a repeating task keeps its repeater, and marking a task
done drops a plain SCHEDULED.  'S-<right>' and 'S-<left>' go through
every TODO sequence, 'C-c C-l' edits the link at point, links find
headings in any case, tables measure links as they are shown, and
clock durations of a day or more are written correctly.

** Fixes in Org's agenda and capture.
The agenda reads whole files when they are narrowed, and takes a
timestamp's own warning and delay, CATEGORY properties, repeating
deadlines on later dates and date ranges on every day; times sort with
the time grid and the now line.  Capture uses 'org-directory', finds
headings with cookies, updates the parent's cookies, fills '%i' from
the region, keeps '%?' on its line and leaves blank lines as Org does.

** Fixes in files, Dired, compilation, the shell and frames.
'write-file' writes into a directory, and 'find-alternate-file' asks as
Emacs does.  Dired picks the files a prefix argument names and folds
case in its regexps.  Bookmarks delete only what is asked, and
'C-u <f3>' appends to the keyboard macro.  Compilation uses no pager,
follows make's directory changes, shows ':run' and ':exit [N]' in the
mode line, and handles colour codes split between writes.  The shell
shows '(Shell:run)', tracks directories as the shell reads their names,
and 'C-c C-c' keeps the typed command in the history.  'C-x 5 o' passes
over hidden frames, 'C-u C-x 5 1' iconifies the others, and 'C-x 5 f'
and 'C-x t f' take directories.


* Changes in E8 0.5.13

E8 0.5.13 is a bug-fix release with no new features.

** 'C-x k' and 'C-x b' offer their defaults first.
RET takes the default the prompt names, the current buffer for 'C-x k'
and the other buffer for 'C-x b', and vertico lists it first.

** Org's Auto Fill leaves headings, tables, keywords and blocks whole.
'org-mode' sets 'normal-auto-fill-function' to 'org-auto-fill-function'.
Paragraphs keep their indentation, items line up under their text and
comments go on with "# ".

** Org applies ':VISIBILITY:' properties when a file is opened.
They are applied after the '#+STARTUP' folding, as Emacs's Org does.

** Repeated 'M-x undo' goes on undoing when vertico reads the command.

** Org's '?' speed-command help and transient's help are in 'help-mode'.

** 'M-x vundo' opens its window in the GUI.
Splitting a window two or more lines tall no longer fails with "Size of
new window too small" because of the GUI's taller mode line.


* Changes in E8 0.5.12

** The default look uses E8's brand colours, in dark and light.
Midnight, Lavender and Ivory replace the blue palette in both front
ends.  'font-lock-constant-face' and 'font-lock-number-face' use a new
rose colour rather than the colour of errors, and the GUI's visible bell
follows the theme.

** The GUI draws E8's lattice behind the text when its window opens.
It fades within six seconds, or at once at the first key, click or
scroll.  Set 'e8-startup-lattice' to nil, or 'inhibit-startup-screen' to
t, to turn it off.

** The app icon is E8's new rosette.

** The desktop puts the GUI window back where it was, and as big.
With 'desktop-save-mode' on and 'desktop-restore-frames' t, the window's
place and size are saved and restored.  'window-system' is now set while
'init.ts' and the desktop are read in the GUI.

** Flyspell no longer makes typing wait for the spelling checker.
Answers are remembered, and checks after a command do not wait: the
underline appears a few milliseconds later.  'flyspell-buffer' shares
large regions among up to three more Hunspell processes and does less
work per word.

** Short regexp searches and literal searches are faster.
This helps every package that searches, not only flyspell.

** 'set-frame-size' given 'frame-text-height' no longer shrinks the frame.


* Changes in E8 0.5.11

** New minor mode 'prettify-symbols-mode'.
It shows symbols such as 'lambda' as λ, outside strings and comments;
'global-prettify-symbols-mode' turns it on everywhere.  Compositions
work as Emacs's do, with 'compose-region' and 'decompose-region'.

** 'eval-buffer' and 'eval-region' run the TypeScript being edited.
Text that imports runs as a module.  Both ask the agent's permission
while it acts.

** The GUI shows web pages in buffers on macOS and Linux.
'xwidget-webkit-browse-url' and 'xwidget-webkit-mode' work as Emacs's
xwidgets do.  On Linux the GUI needs WebKitGTK.

** 'C-g' in the minibuffer runs 'abort-minibuffers', as in Emacs 28.
From an outer minibuffer it asks "Abort N minibuffer levels?".  A quit
minibuffer signals 'minibuffer-quit', so a keyboard macro goes on past
one, as in Emacs.

** 'C-h b' and 'C-x C-h' list bindings as Emacs's do.
They have page breaks, tab-padded key columns, runs of characters on one
line, a section for meta keys and one for remaps, and leave out keys
that are shadowed.

** Dates follow the editor's clock and time zone.
Dired, diff and compile write times with 'format-time-string' and
'current-time-string', and Org reads today's date and clock times the
same way, so they follow 'set-time-zone-rule'.

** 'top-level' leaves every nested minibuffer.

** Scripts can use the rest of Emacs's C primitives under their names.
Every editor primitive Emacs writes in C is now in the 'e8' API under
its Emacs name, among them 'format', 'format-message', 'read-variable',
'mark-marker', 'buffer-swap-text', labeled restrictions, POSIX
searches ('posixSearchForward', 'posixStringMatch'), base64,
'compareStrings', 'stringDistance', 'bufferHash', 'prin1' and Lisp's
arithmetic ('expt', 'frexp', 'e8.logarithm').

** Scripts can use SQLite and JSON as Emacs does.
'e8.sqliteOpen', 'sqliteSelect', 'sqliteExecute' and the rest follow
sqlite.c; SQLite is not in the web build.  'e8.jsonParseString',
'jsonParseBuffer', 'jsonSerialize' and 'jsonInsert' follow json.c, with
its options and errors.

** Scripts have Emacs's time functions.
'e8.formatTimeString', 'decodeTime', 'encodeTime', 'set-time-zone-rule'
and the rest read time zones from the system's zone files, as the C
library does.

** Scripts can alias and watch variables.
'e8.defvaralias' and 'addVariableWatcher', with 'boundp', 'internSoft',
'mapatoms' and the rest of Emacs's functions on variables and symbols.

** Scripts can define coding systems and charsets.
'define-coding-system', 'define-coding-system-alias' and 'define-charset'
work as in mule.el.  'coding-system-get' now gives what
'coding-system-put' put.

** Scripts have the rest of Emacs's tree-sitter, face and font functions.
Tree-sitter gains 'e8.treesitSearchSubtree', 'treesitSearchForward',
compiled queries, included ranges ('treesitParserSetIncludedRanges') and
parser notifiers ('treesitParserAddNotifier').  Faces and fonts gain
Lisp faces ('internalMakeLispFace', 'internalSetLispFaceAttribute'),
'internalCharFont', 'setFontsetFont' and 'fontsetInfo'.

** Scripts can watch files, open serial ports and use macOS's functions.
'file-notify-add-watch' and its kin, on kqueue; 'makeSerialProcess' and
'set-network-process-option'; and the NS port's functions, such as
'nsGetSelection'.


* Changes in E8 0.5.10

E8 0.5.10 is a bug-fix release with no new features.

** Scrolling up with the wheel or trackpad is no longer pulled back.
Over lines continued on several rows, 'mwheel-scroll' left point below
the window, and redisplay recentred on it, undoing the scroll.

** Scrolling with flyspell on no longer checks a word at each step.
'mwheel-scroll', 'scroll-up-line' and 'scroll-down-line' are now in
'flyspell-deplacement-commands', so scrolling keeps up.


* Changes in E8 0.5.9

** 'menu-bar-mode' shows a menu bar, which '<f10>' opens.
File, Edit, Options, Buffers, Tools and Help as menu-bar.el has them,
with a mode's own menus, in a terminal as in the window. The arrows and
'RET' walk it, and a click opens the menu under the pointer.

** Pop-up menus, dialogs and tooltips work in a terminal as in a window.
'x-popup-menu', 'x-popup-dialog' and 'message-box' are drawn by E8
itself, and 'tooltip-mode' shows tooltips. 'y-or-n-p' asks in a dialog
when a menu or the mouse asked, unless 'use-dialog-box' is nil.

** windmove and winner are ported from Emacs.
'windmove-default-keybindings' puts 'windmove-left' and its kin on
'S-<left>' and the other arrows; 'windmove-display-*',
'windmove-delete-*' and 'windmove-swap-states-*' show, delete and swap
by direction.
'winner-mode' undoes and redoes window changes with 'C-c <left>' and
'C-c <right>'.

** New commands 'maximize-window' and 'minimize-window'.

** 'C-x <' and 'C-x >' scroll the window sideways.
They run 'scroll-left' and 'scroll-right'. Truncated lines scroll to
keep point in sight as Emacs's do, following 'auto-hscroll-mode',
'hscroll-margin' and 'hscroll-step'.

** 'C-z' and 'C-x C-z' run 'suspend-frame'.
In the GUI it minimizes the window; in a terminal it stops E8 for the
shell, as 'suspend-emacs' does, and 'fg' brings it back.

** Network connections can use TLS, with the Network Security Manager.
'open-network-stream' takes ':type tls', or starts TLS after plain text.
E8 asks about a certificate that does not verify or a key that changed,
and remembers the answer for the session or for good.

** Files can choose their coding system by name.
'file-coding-system-alist' is honoured, as are
'process-coding-system-alist' and 'network-coding-system-alist'; a
file's own coding cookie wins over its name.

** Windows split, resize and delete as Emacs's window.el has them.
'C-x 2' twice gives three windows of 6, 6 and 12 lines on 24, as in
Emacs, and windows keep their shares when the frame is resized.
'balance-windows' and 'fit-window-to-buffer' respect fixed and preserved
sizes, and atomic and side windows behave as in Emacs.

** 'display-buffer' follows 'display-buffer-alist' and its actions.
Emacs's action functions, such as 'display-buffer-in-side-window' and
'display-buffer-reuse-window', and entries such as 'window-height' and
'dedicated' work as in Emacs.

** 'quit-window' restores a window's previous buffer, place and size.
'window-state-get' and 'window-state-put' save and restore a frame's
windows, and the desktop now saves them as Emacs does.

** Windows, frames, processes and terminals are objects in scripts.
'windowp' and 'framep' answer as Emacs's do, and a window prints as
'#<window 1 on *scratch*>'. Functions still take numbers for now.

** Scripts have many more of Emacs's primitives, under Emacs's names.
Among them 'window-frame', 'set-frame-size', 'set-process-sentinel',
'call-process-region', 'vertical-motion', 'user-full-name',
'mouse-position', 'track-mouse' and 'libxml-parse-html-region'.

** Scripts can ask about and change frames, windows and the display.
'set-frame-position', 'frame-parameters', 'set-window-fringes',
'window-configuration-equal-p', 'terminal-parameter',
'x-display-pixel-width' and 'display-monitor-attributes-list' answer
as Emacs's do, and charsets ('split-char', 'find-charset-region') and
'current-idle-time' are there too.

** A terminal shows '$' at the right of a line cut off there.

** A command waiting for an event after a mouse click no longer hangs.

** Fixes to windows and scrolling in the GUI.
The GUI's smooth scrolling stays with the text when it is edited, and
'C-l' resets it. Dragging a divider follows the pointer. A window with
no mode line uses that line for text, so a transient menu's last row
shows again.


* Changes in E8 0.5.8

** 'global-set-key' and 'local-set-key' are commands.
So are 'global-unset-key' and 'local-unset-key'; 'local-set-key' gives
a buffer with no local map one.

** Scripts have more of Emacs's primitives, under Emacs's names.
Keymaps ('current-local-map', 'use-global-map', 'copy-keymap'), syntax
tables ('standard-syntax-table'), overlays from any buffer
('overlay-buffer', 'remove-overlays'), markers' insertion types, and
one-liners such as 'eobp' and 'erase-buffer'.

** Spell checking keeps one checker running, as ispell.el does.
Moving about with Flyspell and Hunspell no longer pauses at each word,
since the checker is no longer started afresh for every check.

** 'accept-process-output', 'sit-for' and 'sleep-for' wake at once.
They return as soon as a process's output comes, which speeds up
talking to programs a line at a time, such as language servers.


* Changes in E8 0.5.7

** Spell checking follows ispell.el, with several languages at once.
With Hunspell, setting 'ispell-dictionary' to "en_US,sv_SE" checks both.
'ispell-change-dictionary', 'ispell-region', 'ispell-buffer',
'ispell-complete-word' and 'ispell-minor-mode' are new, and '*Choices*'
takes all of Emacs's answers.

** File-local variables are set from a file's '-*-' line and its list.
As Emacs's 'hack-local-variables' sets them, asking about unsafe values.
'add-file-local-variable' and its kin write them.

** Directory-local variables are read from '.dir-locals.el'.
And '.dir-locals-2.el', for a mode or a subdirectory, Dired buffers
included. 'add-dir-local-variable' and its kin write them.

** 'C-h h' names a key that a configuration gave to another command.
For example "C-x b  Go to another buffer (now consult-buffer)". The
cards' titles, headings and keys also keep their faces.

** 'before-init-hook' runs after E8's packages load and before init.ts.

** 'expand-file-name' keeps a directory name's trailing slash.

** A directory trusted for its local variables is trusted through links.
'safe-local-variable-directories' is compared after following symbolic
links, as Emacs compares it.


* Changes in E8 0.5.6

** 'C-h h' shows a short guide to the current mode, 'C-h 8' one to E8.
Org, Dired, Prog mode and agent-shell have cards of their own; other
modes get one from their documentation and keymap. 'help-guide-mode'
turns the guides and the startup hint off.

** agent-shell's header line shows only the session's settings.
Its model, mode and thought level. 'agent-shell-header-style' chooses
"text", "graphical" or no header line, and 'agent-shell-current-model'
and its kin hold the settings for a mode line of one's own.


* Changes in E8 0.5.5

E8 0.5.5 is a bug-fix release with no new features.

** A long word is no longer broken right after a line prefix.
With 'org-indent-mode' and 'visual-line-mode', a long URL started with
an empty row, and 'C-n' stuck on it.


* Changes in E8 0.5.4

E8 0.5.4 is a bug-fix release with no new features.

** Underlines are drawn where the font puts them, under their own line.
In the GUI, as Emacs draws them, rather than at the bottom of the row,
close to the next line's text.


* Changes in E8 0.5.3

** The website's E8 can be used on a phone or tablet.
A row of keys (Esc, Ctrl, Alt, Tab and the arrows) and a button that
brings up the keyboard; a swipe scrolls, a tap moves point, and holding
then dragging selects. Org's speed commands are on in its configuration.

** The 'e8-themes' package has 168 themes.

** Themes are drawn as Emacs draws them.
':distant-foreground' is used when a face's foreground is too close to
its background, strike-through crosses the middle of the text, and
'diff-mode' has dark faces. 'load-theme' reads the theme's file again,
so options set before it take effect.

** 'visual-line-mode' shows no continuation arrows in the fringes.

** Transient menus leave 'window-min-height' lines for the window above.


* Changes in E8 0.5.2

** E8 has a website, with E8 running in the page.
At https://e8-editor.github.io/e8/, set up with ports such as Vertico,
Orderless and Consult, and with 'C-c t' to choose a theme. '?plain'
starts it without them, and a button fills the browser's window.

** Themes come as one package, 'e8-themes', with a chooser.
Twenty themes, among them Modus, Leuven, Nord, Gruvbox, Solarized and
Dracula. 'e8-themes-select' shows each as the selection moves.

** 'load-theme' completes over every available theme.
'custom-available-themes' finds the themes along 'load-path', not only
those already loaded.

** 'visual-line-mode' wraps lines in narrow side-by-side windows.
It makes 'truncate-partial-width-windows' nil in the buffer, as Emacs's
does.


* Changes in E8 0.5.1

** agent-shell resumes earlier sessions.
'M-x agent-shell' offers the project's earlier sessions, Claude Code's
own included, and replays the chosen one with earlier replies folded.
'agent-shell-session-strategy' says whether to ask. An agent that stops
is started again in the same session.

** agent-shell recalls earlier requests with 'M-p', 'M-n' and 'M-r'.
'agent-shell-history' is shared by every agent-shell buffer, and kept
between starts by 'savehist-mode'.

** agent-shell completes slash commands at the prompt.
TAB or 'C-M-i' after '/' completes the agent's commands, and the echo
area says what the command does.

** Org's speed commands move about agent-shell's transcript.
'org-use-speed-commands' may now be a function, as in Emacs.

** Package upgrades fetch in the background.
'package-vc-upgrade' and 'package-vc-upgrade-all' fetch every package
at once without freezing E8, upgrade what a package requires first, and
upgrade a requirement installed at too old a version.

** E8 can run in a web browser, as a build of its own.
It runs scripts on QuickJS and keeps files in the browser's storage.

** Each buffer has its own 'default-directory'.
A buffer visiting a file takes the file's directory, and a new buffer
the current buffer's, as in Emacs, so programs such as git run from a
file's buffer start in its directory.

** Dired shows a new listing's details until '(' hides them.


* Changes in E8 0.5.0

** Ediff compares and merges two or three files or buffers.
'ediff-files', 'ediff-buffers', 'ediff-files3', 'ediff-buffers3',
'ediff-merge-files' and 'ediff-merge-buffers', with and without an
ancestor, and Ediff's keys and faces.

** Transient menus have levels, saved values and argument history.
'C-x l' sets a suffix's level and 'C-x a' shows every level; 'C-x s'
and 'C-x C-s' set and save a menu's values; 'C-M-p' and 'C-M-n' go
through earlier arguments. 'C-h' on a suffix shows its help.

** The agent-shell buffer is a read-only transcript.
Only the request being typed can be edited or undone, so the buffer
always matches what the agent holds.

** agent-shell shows tool output and thinking in named blocks.
Tool output is a source block in its language, such as 'diff' or
'json', and thinking a folded '#+begin_thinking' block that TAB opens.


* Changes in E8 0.4.1

** Org has 'org-hide-emphasis-markers', off by default.
When it is on, emphasized text such as ~code~ and *bold* is shown
without its markers. The 'agent-shell' buffer turns it on locally.

** New variable 'agent-shell-hidden-tools'.
It names tool calls the 'agent-shell' buffer leaves out. By default
these are Claude Code's bookkeeping calls, 'ToolSearch' and 'TodoWrite'.

** Startup shows what it is doing.
The window is drawn before 'init.ts' runs, and a message from code that
has run for a while, such as installing packages, is shown at once.

** Packages named in 'e8-lock.json' are cloned all at once.
On a machine that has the lock file but not the packages, the first
'e8.usePackage' clones them together and shows its progress.

** 'agent-shell' folds tool output and thinking while the agent streams.
TAB on a line opens its block, which then stays open. A call that asks
permission shows its diff while it asks, and folds once answered.

** 'agent-shell' tool lines are shorter.
A command is followed by Claude Code's description of it, an MCP tool
reads as 'e8: screen', and paths leave out the project's directory.

** 'C-M-\' calls 'indent-region-function' when a mode sets it.
In 'org-mode' it runs 'org-indent-region', so list bodies, property
drawers and source block code are indented as Org indents them.

** Faces are drawn for the theme's background, not the system's.
A dark theme on a Mac in light mode no longer gets light-background
colours. 'frame-background-mode' is now unset by default.

** Indenting a long Org section takes time in proportion to its length.

** Org's region indenting no longer moves a section to the margin.
A blank line after a heading was taken for the heading itself.


* Changes in E8 0.4.0

** E8 has Emacs's help system.
'C-h k', 'C-h c', 'C-h f', 'C-h x', 'C-h v', 'C-h o', 'C-h m', 'C-h w',
'C-h b', 'C-h a' and 'C-h ?' work, '<f1>' is 'C-h', and 'C-h' after a
prefix key lists the keys under it.

** '*Help*' is in 'help-mode', with links and history.
RET follows a name, TAB and S-TAB move between names, and 'l' and 'r'
go back and forward through what was shown.

** New commands 'apropos', 'apropos-command' and 'apropos-variable'.

** 'agent-shell' can set the session's model, mode and thought level.
Use 'agent-shell-set-session-model' ('C-c C-v'),
'agent-shell-set-session-thought-level' ('C-c C-t'),
'agent-shell-cycle-session-mode' ('C-c TAB') and
'agent-shell-set-session-config-option' ('C-c C-s'). The header line
shows the current settings and the keys.

** 'agent-shell' shows the agent's Markdown as Org.
Code becomes source blocks, and bold, italic, code and links become
Org's markup. Tool calls show their command as Org code.

** New variable 'mode-line-process'.
'agent-shell' uses it to show whether the agent is working and how
much of its context is used.

** 'agent-shell' folds tool output into the tool call's line.
TAB on the line opens or folds it, and a failed call says so on its
line. The agent's thinking folds into its 'Thinking:' line.

** 'agent-shell' starts the session when its buffer opens.
Settings can be picked before the first request.

** Vertico, Consult, Magit and other ports are packages of their own.
Orderless, Marginalia, Magit-section, with-editor, Org-roam,
consult-org-roam, Casual, Popper and persistent-scratch too: each is
installed with 'e8.usePackage("e8-editor/e8-NAME")'.

** 'sleep-for' and 'sit-for' wait without reading input in batch mode.


* Changes in E8 0.3.1

** E8 can be installed with Nix, from the 'e8-editor/nix' flake.

** E8.app shows its own icon in the Dock and the app switcher.


* Changes in E8 0.3.0

** The editor is named E8.
The program is 'e8', the window is 'E8.app', the configuration lives in
'~/.config/e8', and the script API's global is 'e8'.

** E8 installs with Homebrew.
'brew install e8-editor/tap/e8' gives 'e8' and 'e8-mcp', and 'E8.app'
on a Mac.

** E8.app has E8's icon.

** New command 'agent-shell' talks to coding agents.
It uses the Agent Client Protocol, as Emacs's agent-shell does.
Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex or Goose work in an Org buffer:
'C-c RET' sends a request and 'C-c C-k' stops the agent.

** 'agent-shell' gives its agents E8's own commands through 'e8-mcp'.
Turning 'agent-shell-e8-tools' off stops this.

** Agents using the open editor through 'e8-mcp' ask before acting.
E8 asks before such an agent saves, discards unsaved work, quits or
runs code: 'y' allows once, '!' for as long as E8 runs.

** 'e8-mcp --socket FILE' connects to the editor listening on FILE.

** New variables 'invocation-directory' and 'invocation-name'.

** 'make-process' can send standard error to a buffer of its own.

** E8.app carries 'e8-mcp'.


* Changes in E8 0.2.2

** Nord is a package of its own.
It is installed with 'e8.usePackage("e8-editor/e8-nord-theme")'.

** A process's last output is no longer lost on a busy machine.
Shell, compile and process buffers could miss a program's last output
when the process ended.


* Changes in E8 0.2.1

** Releases include E8.app for Apple silicon Macs.


* Changes in E8 0.2.0

** vundo is a package of its own.
It is installed with 'e8.usePackage("e8-editor/e8-vundo")'.

** New function 'e8.executeKbdMacro', as Emacs's 'execute-kbd-macro'.

** 'e8 --batch -L DIR' puts DIR at the front of 'load-path'.

** Batch mode lays windows out at 80 by 24, as Emacs's does.


* Changes in E8 0.1.0

E8 0.1.0 is the first release.

** E8 runs in a terminal ('e8') and in a window ('e8-gui').
Releases carry 'e8' and 'e8-mcp' for Apple silicon Macs and Linux.

** Buffers, windows, frames and tabs work as in Emacs.
'C-x 2', 'C-x 3', 'C-x o', 'C-x 4' and 'C-x 5', 'display-buffer',
window configurations, and 'tab-bar-mode' with 'C-x t'.

** Files are visited, saved and backed up as Emacs does it.
Auto-saving and 'recover-this-file', 'auto-revert-mode', lock files,
other codings and line ends, 'recentf-mode' and 'find-file-at-point'.

** Dired, with dired-aux, dired-x and wdired.

** Projects: 'C-x p f', 'C-x p p', 'C-x p b', 'C-x p g' and 'C-x p c'.

** Emacs's editing commands and keys.
The kill ring and 'M-y', the mark ring, registers, rectangles, undo
with 'undo-redo', keyboard macros, abbrevs, 'M-/', filling and
'auto-fill-mode', sexp and paragraph motion, and 'C-x z'.

** Search and replace with Emacs's regexps.
'C-s', 'C-r', 'C-M-s', 'M-%', 'C-M-%', 'replace-regexp', 'occur' and
'M-s h r'.

** The minibuffer completes with Emacs's completion styles.
'icomplete-mode' is on by default, with 'fido-mode' and the vertical
modes, and 'M-p' and 'M-n' go through the history.

** Org mode, with its agenda and capture.
Folding, structure editing, TODO states, tags, tables, links,
refiling, clocking, editing source blocks in their language's mode,
tangling, habits and 'org-indent-mode'.

** A shell in a buffer with 'M-x shell', and shell commands on 'M-!'.

** 'M-x compile' and 'M-x grep' run in the background.
'next-error' on 'M-g n' visits each message's place.

** Tree-sitter modes and Emacs Lisp mode.
'rust-ts-mode', 'js-ts-mode', 'json-ts-mode', 'python-ts-mode',
'go-ts-mode' and 'bash-ts-mode' highlight and indent by Emacs's rules,
and 'emacs-lisp-mode' indents as Emacs does.

** Language servers through 'M-x eglot'.

** Spell checking with 'M-$' and 'flyspell-mode'.
It runs ispell, aspell or hunspell.

** Faces, themes, images and bidirectional text.
'load-theme' colours both front ends; images show in the window, and
Hebrew and Arabic are reordered as Emacs reorders them.

** The mouse clicks, drags, selects and scrolls the window under it.

** Sessions are kept with 'desktop-save-mode'.
'savehist-mode' keeps the history and 'save-place-mode' each file's
place between sessions.

** 'M-x agent' talks to Claude in an Org buffer.
Claude calls E8's commands as tools, and asks before it writes files
or quits. Each turn is one undo step.

** 'e8-mcp' lets Claude Code and other MCP clients use E8.

** E8 is configured and extended in TypeScript.
'init.ts' defines commands and keys, 'M-x load-file' reloads it, and
'M-:' evaluates an expression.

** Packages install from git repositories, as with package-vc.
'e8.usePackage' in 'init.ts', 'package-vc-install',
'package-vc-upgrade' and 'package-delete'; 'e8-lock.json' pins each
package's commit.

** Batch mode and ERT, as 'emacs --batch' and Emacs's ERT.
'e8 --batch -l FILE -f FUNCTION' runs a package's tests, and 'M-x ert'
runs them in the editor.

** Help on 'C-h f', 'C-h k', 'C-h v', 'C-h m', 'C-h b' and 'C-h l'.
